"Here is the most useful fact of all: the height of the North Star above your horizon equals your latitude. At the North Pole, latitude 90 degrees, Polaris is straight overhead. At the equator, latitude 0, it sits right on the horizon. In Changhua, about 24 degrees north, it is about 24 degrees up. The reason is the curve of Earth: as you travel north, your horizon tilts, and the North Star climbs higher. Sailors used this for centuries. The navigators of Zheng He's fleet measured the North Star's height with wooden boards, in units they called \"fingers,\" to know how far north their ships had sailed.": "7140cc8c43de",
"How can you find the North Star when the Big Dipper is below the horizon?": "325c95ea981f",
"How high is the North Star above the horizon in Changhua, and why?": "d975ee703ad5",
"Long before phones and GPS, travelers found their way at night with a single star. On a clear night in Changhua, face north and look about two and a half fists above the horizon. There you will find a star that is not especially bright, yet it does something no other star does: it stays in the same place all night, while the rest of the sky slowly turns around it. This is Polaris, the North Star. Confucius once compared a good ruler to the pole star, which stays in its place while all the other stars turn toward it.": "964ae1cc0799",
"Long before phones and GPS, travelers found their way at night with a single star. On a clear night in Changhua, face north and look about two and a half fists above the horizon. There you will find a star that is not especially bright, yet it does something no other star does: it stays in the same place all night, while the rest of the sky slowly turns around it. This is Polaris, the North Star. Confucius once compared a good ruler to the pole star, which stays in its place while all the other stars turn toward it. Why does it stay still? Earth spins once a day around its axis, an imaginary line through the North and South Poles. Follow that axis out into space from the North Pole, and it lands less than one degree from Polaris. Like the hub of a spinning wheel, Polaris barely moves, while the other stars travel in circles around it: small circles close to it, and big circles farther away. Facing north, you see them turn counterclockwise, about 15 degrees, or one and a half fists, every hour. Here is the most useful fact of all: the height of the North Star above your horizon equals your latitude. At the North Pole, latitude 90 degrees, Polaris is straight overhead. At the equator, latitude 0, it sits right on the horizon. In Changhua, about 24 degrees north, it is about 24 degrees up. The reason is the curve of Earth: as you travel north, your horizon tilts, and the North Star climbs higher. Sailors used this for centuries. The navigators of Zheng He's fleet measured the North Star's height with wooden boards, in units they called \"fingers,\" to know how far north their ships had sailed. Polaris is not the brightest star in the sky; dozens of stars outshine it. The easy way to find it is with the Big Dipper, seven bright stars shaped like a ladle. The two stars at the end of the bowl are the Pointers: draw a line through them and go about five times the distance between them, and you arrive at Polaris. In Taiwan, the Big Dipper sinks below the northern horizon on autumn and winter evenings. Then use Cassiopeia, a bright W on the other side of Polaris. The North Star sits about halfway between the two. The Big Dipper is also a clock and a calendar. It swings around Polaris once a day, and it sits a little farther around at the same hour each night as the seasons change. An old Chinese saying tells where its handle points in the early evening: east in spring, south in summer, west in autumn, and north in winter. From Changhua, the winter position is below the horizon, because at our latitude the Big Dipper sets for part of each night. The 3D model lets you test the saying on any date. The North Star has not always been the North Star. Earth's axis wobbles slowly, like a spinning top, and traces a big circle in the sky every 26,000 years. When the pyramids of Egypt were built, the pole star was Thuban, in the constellation Draco. In Confucius's time, Polaris was about 15 degrees from the pole, so the pole star he admired was a different star. Polaris will come closest to the pole around the year 2100, and in about 12,000 years bright Vega will take its turn. South of the equator, Polaris drops out of sight, and people use the Southern Cross to find south instead.": "fd01ed563ebd",
"Polaris is not the brightest star in the sky; dozens of stars outshine it. The easy way to find it is with the Big Dipper, seven bright stars shaped like a ladle. The two stars at the end of the bowl are the Pointers: draw a line through them and go about five times the distance between them, and you arrive at Polaris. In Taiwan, the Big Dipper sinks below the northern horizon on autumn and winter evenings. Then use Cassiopeia, a bright W on the other side of Polaris. The North Star sits about halfway between the two.": "4fcad00588d6",
